AoU-LR patch to FELIX v0.1 (lhu1/felix@sha256:63c1e1d63aa029bc00403547b05554448c71d3b64164a16b93acb66a0ebeab88).

Stop an extreme p-value from killing a whole step-2 chunk. In
SPA_ER_kernel_related_Phiadj_fast_new_cpp the upper-tail chi-square quantile
is inverted twice -- once per ancestry, once for the burden/hom p-value --
with no guard on p. boost::math::quantile dispatches to gamma_q_inv, which
throws "Overflow Error" rather than returning an infinite quantile when p is
0, and the exception leaves Rcpp as an R error that halts step 2.

p reaches 0 by a parse failure, not by a modelling failure:
convertStringtoDoublePval catches std::stod's std::out_of_range and returns 0
for any p-value string that underflows double (below ~1e-308), i.e. for the
most strongly associated markers in a scan. Observed on the AoU-LR repeat
dosage arm: FelixGenome RuStep2 for phenotype BI_177_4 died on chr3, chr17
and chr20 with "Error in function boost::math::gamma_q_inv<double>(double,
double): Overflow Error", immediately preceded by two "Argument is out of
range for a double" lines from convertStringtoDoublePval.

Both call sites now go through aou_chisq_upper_quantile, which returns
+infinity at p <= 0, 0 at p >= 1, NaN at NaN, and otherwise hands p to boost
unchanged -- so no marker that currently succeeds changes value. An infinite
qval is what the surrounding code was written for: it zeroes the entries
where VarS becomes infinite and guards VarQ_2 == 0 with r = 1.0.

Apply from /app with: patch -p1 < 0002-chisq-quantile-overflow.patch

